# No LINQ in Runtime Code

Do **not** use `System.Linq` in runtime gameplay code. Every LINQ operator allocates (enumerators, closures, intermediate collections), and heavy generic instantiation bloats IL2CPP output — both are unacceptable in GC-constrained WebGL builds.

## Concrete replacements

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — allocates enumerator + closure per call
var alive = units.Where(u => u.Health > 0).ToList();
var first = units.FirstOrDefault(u => u.Id == id);
bool any = units.Any(u => u.IsBoss);
int total = units.Sum(u => u.Score);

// ✅ GOOD — zero-alloc loops
_aliveBuffer.Clear();
for (int i = 0; i < units.Count; i++)
{
    if (units[i].Health > 0)
    {
        _aliveBuffer.Add(units[i]);
    }
}

Unit first = null;
for (int i = 0; i < units.Count; i++)
{
    if (units[i].Id == id)
    {
        first = units[i];
        break;
    }
}

bool any = false;
for (int i = 0; i < units.Count; i++)
{
    if (units[i].IsBoss)
    {
        any = true;
        break;
    }
}

int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < units.Count; i++)
{
    total += units[i].Score;
}
```

- `Where` + `ToList` → reused buffer list + `for` loop with `Clear()`
- `First` / `FirstOrDefault` → `for` loop with `break`
- `Any` / `All` → `for` loop with early exit
- `Select` → write results into a pre-sized buffer
- `OrderBy` → `List<T>.Sort(comparison)` with a **cached** (static readonly) comparison delegate
- `Count(predicate)` → counter in a `for` loop

## Allowed exceptions

- **Editor code** (`Editor/` folders, editor asmdefs) — tooling clarity wins.
- **Tests** — readability wins.
- **One-time initialization** at load/bootstrap where the caller explicitly documents it is not a hot path — still prefer loops when trivial.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `using System.Linq;` in new or edited runtime files
- [ ] Filtering/searching uses `for` loops with reused buffers
- [ ] Sorting uses `List<T>.Sort` with a cached comparison
- [ ] Any remaining LINQ is in editor code or tests only
